        "input": "An agent calls get_adsorption_sites_text on a Cu(111) slab with cell parameters a = b = 5.12 \u00c5, c = 25.00 \u00c5 (where c includes ~12 \u00c5 vacuum). The tool returns:\n\n{\n  \"ontop\": [\n    [1.28, 0.74, 18.52],\n    [3.84, 2.22, 18.52],\n    [2.56, 1.48, 18.52]\n  ],\n  \"hollow_fcc\": [\n    [2.56, 1.48, 18.45],\n    [0.00, 0.00, 18.45]\n  ],\n  \"hollow_hcp\": [\n    [1.28, 2.22, 18.45],\n    [3.84, 0.74, 18.45]\n  ],\n  \"bridge\": [\n    [2.56, 0.37, 18.50],\n    [1.92, 1.11, 18.50]\n  ]\n}\n\nThe agent must pass one of these coordinates to add_adsorbate_to_slab_text. Which interpretation of this output is correct?",
        "target_scores": {
          "The coordinates are in fractional units; values like 18.52 for z mean the site is at 18.52/25.00 = 0.74 fractional height, which is near the top of the slab. The agent should convert these to Cartesian before passing to add_adsorbate_to_slab_text.": 0,
          "The z-coordinates are identical for all site types (they only appear different due to floating-point rounding); all adsorption sites by definition lie in the same geometric plane exactly at the surface.": 0,
